WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.660
Patty Jenkins' Wonder Woman movie is a pretty
great standalone origin story for the iconic

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:10.740
superhero, and includes some very cool easter
eggs and homages to the comics, the DC Extended

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.460
Universe and, of course, Wonder Woman's previous
incarnations on the small screen.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.660
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I'm going to peel back

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.820
the layers of the new Wonder Woman movie to
uncover some awesome easter eggs, references

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.119
and little details you might have missed.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:28.940
If there's any others you spotted or enjoyed,
leave a comment below and make sure you're

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:33.860
also subscribed for a chance to win one of
two fantastic bundles, each of which includes

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.650
the official movie novelization and "The Art
and Making of Wonder Woman", which showcases

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.530
the gorgeous concept art, designs, sketches
and storyboards from the film.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.910
Spoilers, of course, lie ahead for the movie,
so be aware of that, otherwise, you can check

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.040
out my video of Wonder Woman at Comic Con
by tapping in the top right and come back

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.650
here after you've seen the movie.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:58.360
Wonder Woman director Patty Jenkins has described
the movie as Superman and Indiana Jones meets

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.290
Casablanca, and there is definitely a nice
nod to Richard Donner's 1978 Superman movie

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.630
during the alley scene when Diana stops a
bullet that's about to hit Steve Trevor, and

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.300
the bullet falls into the palm of Steve's
hand.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.460
This moment recalls how Clark Kent saved Lois
Lane in an alley from a mugger's gunshot by

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.780
catching the bullet in the palm of his hand.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.570
When Steve reveals his identity to the Amazons,
under the influence of the Lasso of Truth,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.540
he tells them his serial number is 81 41 921,
which is a subtle easter egg to the comic

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.380
book where both Wonder Woman and Steve Trevor
made their first appearance: volume 1, issue

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.490
8 of All-Star Comics, December to January
1941 to 1942.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:44.120
Just before Diana leaves London for the Front
with Steve, there's a rather sweet moment

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.659
at the train station where she tastes ice
cream for the first time.

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:52.031
This scene is a call-back to a very similar
moment in the 2011 Justice League: Origin

00:01:52.031 --> 00:01:55.700
comic where Wonder Woman got her first taste
of ice cream.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.260
And in the new movie, she even tells the vendor,
"you should be very proud", just as she does

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.550
in the comic.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:06.840
By the way, this moment was also recreated
in the 2014 animated movie, Justice League:

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:07.840
War.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.600
To stop Diana revealing herself as the Princess
of Themyscira, Steve Trevor hastily introduces

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.390
her as Diana Prince.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:19.451
In the comics, Diana Prince was the secret
alias that Diana first took on in 1942 when

00:02:19.451 --> 00:02:23.790
she met a nurse with that name who looked
just like her, but wore glasses.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.980
And in the movie, when Diana puts on a pair
of glasses as part of her new identity, that's

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.860
also a hat-tip to that same comic book story.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.140
By the way, Patty Jenkins has said she included
several subtle homages to actress Lynda Carter's

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.740
Wonder Woman from the 1970s TV series.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:44.050
For example, when Diana translates Dr Poison's
notebook for the army generals, Steve lies

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.780
to them, saying Diana is his secretary, which
is a hint to the 70s TV show where Diana Prince

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.520
was indeed Steve Trevor's secretary.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.099
And in the movie, when Steve insists Diana
wear glasses so that she looks 'less distracting',

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:01.350
it's a throwback to the TV pilot for the 70s
series, in which Steve says that after all

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.490
the trouble he's had with "pretty girls",
he wants an "ordinary-looking secretary",

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.310
after which he meets a glasses-wearing Diana
Prince.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:14.050
At the department store, when Steve asks Etta
Candy where Diana is, Etta replies that she's

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.510
"trying on outfit number 226", which is an
interesting number for Etta to pluck out of

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.569
the air as in the Wonder Woman comics, the
cover of issue 226 features Superman raising

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.440
a tank above his head, much like the moment
in the movie when Wonder Woman does the same.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.090
There's a shout-out to Paradise Island, the
original name of Wonder Woman's homeland in

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:39.430
the comics and the 1970s TV show, when Steve
asks Diana if they have dancing on Paradise

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.430
Island.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:44.879
By the way, the name Themyscira, which is
used for Diana's homeland in the movie, appeared

00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:46.390
in later comics.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.870
The humorous moment on the boat when Diana
tells Steve she's read all 12 of Clio's books

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:55.380
on Bodily Pleasure is a reference to the royal
Amazon scribe called Clio who appeared in

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.540
the Wonder Woman comics.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:01.110
There's an interesting call-back to the prevalent
use of chains in the Wonder Woman comics during

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:06.560
Diana's final battle with Ares when the God
of War manages to momentarily chain her arm.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.860
In the early Wonder Woman comics, welding
chains to an Amazon's bracelets could temporarily

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.490
weaken them, though this changed in later
comics, so Ares' use of a chain as a weapon

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.269
against Diana and her breaking free from it
are symbolic.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:23.790
Any Wonder Woman fan will, of course, be familiar
with her Lasso of Truth, but in the movie

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:28.139
Diana calls it the Lasso of Hestia, which
is a reference to the Goddess of the Hearth

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:31.830
and Home, Hestia, whose fires gave the lasso
its power.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.820
Wonder Woman links back to Batman v Superman
where her character first appeared in the

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.639
DCEU when modern-day Diana who's working at
the Louvre receives a package from Wayne Enterprises.

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:45.770
In the package is the photo of her, Steve,
Sameer, Charlie, and the Chief that Bruce

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.110
Wayne stole from Lex Luthor in BvS.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.300
And there's also a little tease to the Justice
League in the letters "JL" which appear on

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.000
the license plate of the Wayne Enterprises
van that delivers that photo.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.889
Before Wonder Woman defeats Ares, he makes
a proposition to her that has strong echoes

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:03.039
of Man of Steel.

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:08.819
Ares asks Diana to join him and rid the world
of humankind, and she responds, "I can never

00:05:08.819 --> 00:05:13.419
be a part of that", a clear call-back to how
Superman refused Zod's proposition to help

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:16.559
him with the World Engine on Earth with the
words, "No, Zod.

00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:18.610
I can't be a part of this."

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:23.300
Diana, of course, finally defeats Ares but
it turns out that it's not her sword that's

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.629
the God Killer, but herself, which is a really
interesting easter egg because, in the comics,

00:05:27.629 --> 00:05:32.389
the God Killer sword was actually wielded
by Deathstroke in a story that also featured

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:33.389
Wonder Woman.

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:37.170
But what's even more intriguing is that the
way Wonder Woman defeats Ares in the movie

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:41.710
by taking the electrical energy he blasts
at her and powering it back at him is very

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.840
reminiscent of how the God Killer sword in
the comics can take the energy it receives

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.569
from an aggressor and turn it back on them
with double the force.

